<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fan Noli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Theofan Stilian Noli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fan Noli was an Albanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, writer and politician. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> he founded an independent Albanian Orthodox church and became its bishop. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/june-revolution-albania">June Revolution</a> of 1924 and was prime minister of Albania for six months until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zog-i">Ahmet Zogu</a> overthrew him that December. He spent the rest of his life in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, where he translated Shakespeare and other classics into Albanian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fan-noli.jpg" alt="Fan Noli" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fan_Stilian_Noli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
